      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I believe this also affects the private range too?\n\nWell actually it doesn\u0027t, see how `hyp_s1_pgtable_pages()`\u0027s behaviour only differs for the memblock parsing part, but we still do a normal reservation for a 1G private range. I thought we\u0027d want to keep that, to avoid confusing a hyp s1 mapping failure with the hyp_alloc ENOMEM case for ex.\n\n\u003e I wonder if that wouldn\u0027t be easier to reason with \"the number of pages we can map in the hyp stage-1\" but then computing the worst case is a bit annoying and I do sympathize this is a \"temporary\" solution. So wouldn\u0027t that be easier to just define that as the size of the hyp stage-1 memory directly?\n\nYeah I totally agree that the value as-is is a bit difficult to define precisely, I just couldn\u0027t find anything simple that was much better :( I did consider just making this \u0027the size of the hyp stage-1 reservation\u0027, but I thought that was even harder to reason about, because it\u0027s even further away from what you\u0027d really want to express. I find it difficult (intuitively at least) to reason about how much memory can be mapped with a page-table of size X. So `hyp_lm_size_mb` is kind of a proxy for the max size of the linear map, which is equally dodgy, but fits a little better my mental model.\n\nFor all options I could think of, the only way to set it \u0027properly\u0027 was through trial and error, so it probably doesn\u0027t matter all that much?",
